;; https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-string.prototype.split, over an already-coerced receiver and a
;; separator that is either a string or absent.
;;
;; This remains a macro for graph visibility. Heap-touching builtins are also legal: JSL gives one
;; an implicit trailing memory argument, threads it through nested calls, and returns the resulting
;; effect token to the caller. Recursive JSON parsing uses that path; split benefits more from
;; inlining its single loop.
;;
;; AN ABSENT SEPARATOR IS A SEPARATE DEFINITION, not a flag. The three cases are genuinely
;; different — `"abc".split()` is `["abc"]`, `"abc".split("")` is `["a","b","c"]`, and
;; `"abc".split("b")` is `["a","c"]` — and no separator VALUE distinguishes the first from the
;; second: comparing against `undefined` would compare a raw string pointer against a tagged word
;; and never match. Passing the argument count in as an `int` works for every other case and fails
;; for that one: the count is a literal 0 at the call site, so the mode arithmetic folds during
;; construction and leaves a Region and its Phi disagreeing — `VERR-ARITY`, the same builder defect
;; `"ab".repeat(0)` hits. Two definitions have no flag to fold. The argument count is a property of
;; the CALL SITE and the frontend already knows it, which is the reasoning `push` uses for its
;; argument loop.
;;
;; THERE IS ONE LOOP AND ONE STORE, AND THAT SHAPE IS FORCED. Written the readable way — an `if` on
;; the argument count around an `if` on the separator length, each arm allocating and writing — it
;; does not compile: a merge whose two arms both wrote the heap leaves a memory Phi that never gets
;; a computed type, and selection refuses it with `MSEL-UNSUPPORTED` on a `Phi` still carrying ANY.
;; Every other heap-touching definition in the library happens to write on only one arm of any
;; branch it contains, so nothing had exercised it. Collapsing the three cases into one body, with
;; the store ahead of the only branch, keeps every merge here a merge of the same heap.
;;
;; The three cases are then just two numbers. `seplen` is -1 when the separator is absent, which
;; makes `hit` -1 on the first pass and emits the whole string; 0 for the empty separator, which
;; steps one code unit at a time; and the real length otherwise. The bound differs with them: the
;; empty separator STOPS at the end, where the other two run once more so the tail after the last
;; separator is emitted — that is what makes `"a,".split(",")` two elements and `"".split(",")` one.
;;
;; THE ELEMENTS ARE BOXED. Array elements are stored boxed, and `%Substring` answers a raw string
;; pointer, so every piece is boxed on the way in — the same rule the read paths rely on when they
;; compare stored words.
;; EVERY MODE DECISION IS HOISTED OUT OF THE LOOP, as three integers rather than three tests. It is
;; not only smaller: a branch inside a loop body that also writes the heap is the shape that keeps
;; failing here, and the fewer of them the body has the further it gets. `unit` is 1 for the empty
;; separator and 0 otherwise, which turns both the end of the piece and the stopping bound into
;; arithmetic — `slen - unit < from` is `from >= slen` for the empty separator and `from > slen` for
;; the other two, which is exactly the difference between stopping at the end and running once more
;; to emit the tail.
(builtin StringSplitWhole :transitioning true :params [(s dyn)] :ret dyn
  (let [(out (%NewArray))
        (ignored (%ArrayStore out 0 (%Box s)))]
    out))

(builtin StringSplit :transitioning true :params [(s dyn) (sep dyn)] :ret dyn
  (let [(out (%NewArray))
        (slen (%StringLen s))
        (seplen (%StringLen sep))
        (unit (if (%Eq seplen 0) 1 0))
        (step (if (%Eq seplen 0) 1 seplen))
        (limit (%Sub slen unit))]
    (loop [(from 0) (n 0)]
      (if (%Lt limit from)
          out
          ;; An empty needle matches AT `from`, so ONE `%StringIndexOf` serves the empty separator
          ;; and the general case both; `unit` is what turns that hit into `from + 1` rather than
          ;; `from`. With the absent separator gone to its own definition there is no branch here.
          (let [(hit (%StringIndexOf s sep from))
                (end (if (%Lt hit 0) slen (%Add hit unit)))
                (ignored (%ArrayStore out n (%Box (%Substring s from end))))]
            (if (%Lt hit 0) out (recur (%Add hit step) (%Add n 1))))))))
